    ("Henri Toulouse-Lautrec: Ambassadeurs: Aristide Bruant", "Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec", 1892, "Color lithograph poster", "Post-Impressionism / French", ["poster","bold outlines","flat color","graphic","cabaret"], ["Aristide Bruant","poster","cabaret","bold red scarf","hat"], ["red scarf","black hat","yellow","bold outlines"], "Various collections", "A massive bold figure in black hat and red scarf fills the poster for the cabaret singer Aristide Bruant.", "Lautrec revolutionized poster art. His flat color and bold silhouettes made him the father of modern graphic design.", "male", "French"),
    ("Édouard Vuillard: Woman in Blue", "Édouard Vuillard", 1905, "Oil on cardboard", "Intimisme / Post-Impressionism", ["intimate","patterned","domestic","small scale","absorbed"], ["woman","interior","blue dress","wallpaper","absorbed"], ["blue","patterned wallpaper","cream","domestic"], "Various collections", "A woman in a blue dress is nearly absorbed into the patterned wallpaper and tablecloth that surrounds her.", "Vuillard's intimist works reduce domestic space to pure surface pattern. The woman and her environment merge into a single decorative unity.", "male", "French"),
    ("Pierre Bonnard: Nude in the Bath", "Pierre Bonnard", 1936, "Oil on canvas", "Post-Impressionism / French", ["colorful","nude","domestic","pattern","intimate"], ["nude woman","bathtub","blue tiles","domestic","intimate"], ["turquoise","pink","cream","warm"], "Musée d'Art Moderne, Paris", "His wife Marthe immersed in a bathtub surrounded by brilliant tile color, her body translucent in the warm water.", "Bonnard's bathing Marthe series (she was obsessed with bathing) transforms domestic intimacy into color and light experiments.", "male", "French"),
    ("Camille Pissarro: Boulevard Montmartre at Night", "Camille Pissarro", 1897, "Oil on canvas", "Impressionism / French", ["impressionistic","nocturnal","urban","lights","atmospheric"], ["paris boulevard","night lights","carriages","crowds","wet street"], ["gold gas lights","dark blue","reflection","wet pavement"], "National Gallery, London", "The Boulevard Montmartre at night with gas lights reflected in wet pavement and carriages flowing below.", "Pissarro painted this and other Boulevard Montmartre views from his hotel room window in all weather and light conditions.", "male", "French"),
    ("Alfred Sisley: Flood at Port-Marly", "Alfred Sisley", 1876, "Oil on canvas", "Impressionism / French", ["impressionistic","flood","reflection","peaceful","atmosphere"], ["flood","houses","reflections","sky","trees"], ["pale blue","cream","grey water reflection","gentle"], "Musée d'Orsay, Paris", "Flooded streets of Port-Marly reflect the sky and half-submerged buildings in perfect Impressionist calm.", "Among the most serene Impressionist landscapes. Sisley is the most neglected of the core Impressionists — his flood series is his masterpiece.", "male", "French"),
    ("Berthe Morisot: Summer's Day", "Berthe Morisot", 1879, "Oil on canvas", "Impressionism / French", ["impressionistic","outdoor","loose","social","fresh"], ["two women","boat","lake","parasol","fresh air"], ["blue water","white parasols","green","dappled light"], "National Gallery, London", "Two women relax in a rowing boat on the Bois de Boulogne lake, ducks paddling nearby, in dappled afternoon light.", "Morisot captures the outdoor leisure world of Parisian women with Impressionist freshness. Degas admired her touch above all others.", "female", "French"),

    # ── SYMBOLISM & SECESSION EXPANDED ────────────────────────────────────
    ("Franz von Stuck: The Sin", "Franz von Stuck", 1893, "Oil on canvas", "Symbolism / Munich", ["symbolist","femme fatale","serpent","dark","erotic"], ["Eve","serpent","nude","darkness","temptation"], ["dark","flesh","serpent scales","gold frame"], "Neue Pinakothek, Munich", "A dark-haired nude woman stares out from the dark with heavy-lidded eyes while a massive serpent coils around her shoulders.", "The defining image of the late-19th-century femme fatale. Von Stuck's gilded frames make the paintings into altarpieces for a new religion of beauty.", "male", "German"),
    ("Max Klinger: Glove series", "Max Klinger", 1881, "Etching series", "Symbolism / German", ["etching","dream logic","sequential","anxiety","surrealist precursor"], ["glove","dream","pursuit","woman","strange"], ["black and white etching","crosshatch"], "Various collections", "A series of etchings in which a found glove sets off erotic and threatening dream sequences in a skating rink.", "De Chirico cited Klinger as the first artist to understand that everyday objects could be charged with poetic strangeness. A direct ancestor of Surrealism.", "male", "German"),
    ("Giovanni Segantini: The Evil Mothers", "Giovanni Segantini", 1894, "Oil on canvas", "Symbolism / Italian-Swiss", ["symbolist","alpine","divisionist","mythological","tragic"], ["frozen women","alpine landscape","dead tree","punishment","symbolic"], ["blue ice","white snow","pink sky","winter"], "Kunsthaus Zürich", "Women who rejected motherhood are frozen in ice beneath a dead tree in an alpine wasteland — a symbolist punishment.", "Segantini's divisionist technique and alpine symbolism are unique in European art. His tragic death at 41 cut short an extraordinary career.", "male", "Italian"),
    ("Aubrey Beardsley: The Peacock Skirt (Salomé illustration)", "Aubrey Beardsley", 1894, "Ink on paper", "Art Nouveau / British", ["art nouveau","line drawing","black white","erotic","decorative"], ["Salomé","peacock","elaborate gown","sinuous line","decadent"], ["black ink","white paper","bold patterns"], "Various collections", "Salomé in an elaborate peacock-feather gown with sinuous Art Nouveau line — an illustration for Oscar Wilde's play.", "Beardsley's black-and-white illustrations defined the look of the 1890s Decadence. His line work has never been equaled in its controlled sensuousness.", "male", "British"),

    # ── MODERN EUROPEAN PAINTING ──────────────────────────────────────────
    ("Amedeo Modigliani: Reclining Nude", "Amedeo Modigliani", 1917, "Oil on canvas", "Modernism / Italian", ["nude","elongated","warm","modernist","sensuous"], ["reclining nude","elongated form","warm flesh","closed eyes","direct"], ["ochre","flesh","warm brown","dark background"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "A nude woman reclines with closed eyes, her body elongated into the characteristic Modigliani oval, against a dark background.", "Modigliani's nude exhibition of 1917 was closed by police after one day for obscenity — the first of the 20th century. His nudes show pubic hair, shocking for the era.", "male", "Italian"),
    ("Amedeo Modigliani: Portrait of Jeanne Hébuterne", "Amedeo Modigliani", 1919, "Oil on canvas", "Modernism / Italian", ["portrait","elongated","oval face","neck","intimate"], ["Jeanne","long neck","blank eyes","oval face","intimate"], ["warm ochre","cream","dark background","elongated"], "Various collections", "Modigliani's partner Jeanne Hébuterne with characteristic blank almond eyes, long neck, and compressed oval face.", "Jeanne was his great love and mother of his child. She killed herself the day after Modigliani's death from tuberculosis at 35.", "male", "Italian"),
    ("Marc Chagall: I and the Village", "Marc Chagall", 1911, "Oil on canvas", "Cubism / Jewish-Russian", ["cubist","dreamlike","colorful","village","nostalgic"], ["green face","goat","village","floating figures","memory"], ["green","red","blue","white","circular"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "A large green face gazes into a goat's eye; the village of Vitebsk floats upside-down in the circular composition.", "Chagall's dreamlike memory paintings of his Russian-Jewish childhood created a unique lyrical cubism. Breton called him a born Surrealist.", "male", "Russian"),
    ("Marc Chagall: The Birthday", "Marc Chagall", 1915, "Oil on cardboard", "Cubism / Jewish-Russian", ["joyful","floating","surrealist","intimate","autobiographical"], ["couple","floating","birthday","joy","memory"], ["red","blue","yellow","joyful color"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "Chagall floats upside-down to kiss his beloved Bella on his birthday, defying gravity in pure joy.", "Chagall used levitation throughout his career to signify joy, love, and spiritual transport. This birthday scene is his most directly personal image.", "male", "Russian"),
    ("Robert Delaunay: Simultaneous Windows on the City", "Robert Delaunay", 1912, "Oil on canvas", "Orphism / French", ["orphism","colorful","fragmented","window","abstract"], ["city","Eiffel Tower","color prisms","window","simultaneous"], ["gold","blue","green","red","transparent prisms"], "Kunsthalle Hamburg", "The Eiffel Tower fragments into prisms of simultaneous color through a window — Cubism transformed into pure color sensation.", "Delaunay's Orphism took Cubism's fragmentation and replaced analysis with pure color harmony. It influenced Klee, Macke, and color abstraction.", "male", "French"),
    ("Sonia Delaunay: Electric Prisms", "Sonia Delaunay", 1914, "Oil on canvas", "Orphism / French", ["orphism","colorful","abstract","light","dynamic"], ["electric light","color circles","simultaneous contrast","prisms"], ["red","blue","gold","green","circular rings"], "Centre Pompidou, Paris", "Concentric circles of contrasting color create a visual vibration representing the simultaneity of electric light.", "Sonia Delaunay's large-scale color abstractions were more radical than her husband Robert's. She extended Orphism into fashion, textiles, and book design.", "female", "Russian-French"),
    ("Roger de la Fresnaye: The Conquest of the Air", "Roger de la Fresnaye", 1913, "Oil on canvas", "Cubism / French", ["cubist","lyrical","airplanes","joyful","modern"], ["two figures","balloon","French flag","table","sky"], ["blue","cream","red","light cubist fragments"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "Two men sit at a table; behind them a balloon and French flag rise into the sky in a lyrical Cubist meditation on modern flight.", "Lyrical Cubism — lighter than Picasso or Braque — celebrates rather than analyzes the modern world. The balloon symbolizes France's aviation ambitions.", "male", "French"),
    ("Umberto Boccioni: The City Rises", "Umberto Boccioni", 1910, "Oil on canvas", "Futurism / Italian", ["futurist","dynamic","horse","workers","industrial"], ["horse","workers","city construction","power","movement"], ["red horse","yellow","dark industrial","dramatic"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "A massive red horse rears among construction workers and blurred movement in a city of scaffolding and cranes.", "Boccioni's first major Futurist canvas. The horse represents elemental power in the service of industrial modernity.", "male", "Italian"),
    ("Umberto Boccioni: States of Mind I — The Farewells", "Umberto Boccioni", 1911, "Oil on canvas", "Futurism / Italian", ["futurist","emotional","steam","train","departure"], ["train station","steam","departure","intertwined figures","emotion"], ["dark","steam white","blue green","numbers"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "Steam and numbers from train carriages swirl with the embrace of departing figures in a simultaneous emotional and mechanical space.", "The most psychologically ambitious Futurist painting. The state of mind is rendered through interpenetrating form, color, and symbol.", "male", "Italian"),
    ("Giacomo Balla: Abstract Speed + Sound", "Giacomo Balla", 1913, "Oil on canvas", "Futurism / Italian", ["futurist","abstract","speed","motion","geometric"], ["automobile","speed lines","sound waves","abstract","road"], ["blue","white","line forms","dynamic"], "Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum, New York", "The passage of a car is abstracted into speed lines, noise waves, and dynamic color in pure Futurist energy.", "Balla reduced the automobile to pure abstraction of speed and sound — among the first fully abstract Italian paintings.", "male", "Italian"),

    # ── BAUHAUS & CONSTRUCTIVISM EXPANDED ────────────────────────────────
    ("László Moholy-Nagy: Composition A XXI", "László Moholy-Nagy", 1925, "Oil on canvas", "Constructivism / Bauhaus", ["constructivist","geometric","primary colors","transparent","dynamic"], ["circles","lines","transparency","overlapping forms","light"], ["red","black","white","transparent grey","geometric"], "Peggy Guggenheim Collection, Venice", "Geometric forms including a circle, diagonals, and rectangles overlap in primary colors suggesting transparent planes of light.", "Moholy-Nagy was a key Bauhaus master. His paintings explored the intersection of light, transparency, and geometry — precursors of light installation art.", "male", "Hungarian"),
    ("Herbert Bayer: Lonely Metropolitan", "Herbert Bayer", 1932, "Photomontage", "Bauhaus / Photography", ["photomontage","surrealist","urban","dislocation","hands"], ["floating hands","windows","urban","dislocation","eyes"], ["black and white","grey montage"], "Various collections", "A man's eyes and hands float disembodied at various scales above an urban street scene.", "Bayer's photomontage merges Bauhaus graphic design with Surrealist psychological dislocation. An image of urban alienation that anticipates much later art.", "male", "Austrian"),
    ("Theo van Doesburg: Composition VIII (The Cow)", "Theo van Doesburg", 1918, "Oil on canvas", "De Stijl / Dutch", ["de stijl","abstract","geometric","primary colors","reduction"], ["cow","abstract reduction","geometric planes","primary colors"], ["red","blue","yellow","white","black lines"], "Museum of Modern Art, MoMA", "A series of studies reduced a cow to geometric rectangles of primary color — showing the full process of abstraction from observation.", "Van Doesburg's cow sequence is the most elegant demonstration of the path from naturalism to pure abstraction in early modern art.", "male", "Dutch"),

    # ── ABSTRACT EXPRESSIONISM EXPANDED ────────────────────────────────────
    ("Sam Francis: Towards Disappearance", "Sam Francis", 1958, "Oil on canvas", "Abstract Expressionism / Color Field", ["color field","gestural","edge painting","japanese influence","lyrical"], ["color blobs","edges","white center","flowing paint","open"], ["blue","red","yellow","white center","dripping"], "Various collections", "Splashes of vivid color move around the edges of a vast white canvas, leaving the center open and luminous.", "Francis spent time in Japan and his 'edge paintings' show deep Zen influence. The active edges and empty center invert Western compositional habits.", "male", "American"),
    ("Clyfford Still: PH-950", "Clyfford Still", 1950, "Oil on canvas", "Abstract Expressionism / American", ["color field","jagged","monumental","dark","vertical"], ["jagged color fields","dark","vertical tears","vast","confrontational"], ["dark brown","black","red tears","yellow"], "Clyfford Still Museum, Denver", "Vast dark color fields with jagged vertical tears of raw color — like fissures in rock or burnt earth — rising to the canvas top.", "Still's jagged color shapes are more aggressive than Rothko's soft rectangles. He deliberately refused easy beauty or elegance.", "male", "American"),
    ("Robert Motherwell: Elegy to the Spanish Republic No. 110", "Robert Motherwell", 1971, "Oil on canvas", "Abstract Expressionism / American", ["abstract","gestural","black and white","monumental","political"], ["oval black forms","white ground","political mourning","Spain"], ["black","white","brown tones"], "Cleveland Museum of Art", "Monumental black ovals press against vertical black bars on a white ground — a ritual mourning for the fallen Spanish Republic.", "Motherwell's Elegy series (over 100 paintings) is a sustained abstract dirge. The black ovals suggest both testicular forms and funeral urns.", "male", "American"),
    ("Norman Lewis: Processional", "Norman Lewis", 1965, "Oil on canvas", "Abstract Expressionism / American", ["abstract","figurative traces","atmospheric","civil rights","dark"], ["figures","march","crowd","atmospheric","abstract"], ["dark grey","white","deep blue","ghostly figures"], "Various collections", "Ghostly, barely-legible white figures seem to march through a dark ground — an abstracted Civil Rights march.", "Lewis was the only African American Abstract Expressionist of the core generation. His atmospheric paintings encode Civil Rights imagery in abstraction.", "male", "American"),
    ("Grace Hartigan: Grand Street Brides", "Grace Hartigan", 1954, "Oil on canvas", "Abstract Expressionism / American", ["abstract expressionist","figurative","urban","bold","gestural"], ["wedding dresses","storefronts","urban","figures","gestural"], ["white","cream","red","gestural paint"], "Whitney Museum, New York", "Wedding dresses in a Grand Street shop window dissolve into gestural abstraction — the figures barely readable in slashing paint.", "Hartigan was a major second-generation Abstract Expressionist. Her return to the figure while keeping gestural freedom was path-breaking.", "female", "American"),

    # ── POST-WAR EUROPEAN ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ("Pierre Soulages: Outrenoir", "Pierre Soulages", 1979, "Oil on canvas", "Lyrical Abstraction / French", ["abstract","black","reflective","monochrome","textural"], ["black","light","reflection","texture","monochrome"], ["black on black","reflected light","deep texture"], "Musée Soulages, Rodez", "A vast canvas of black paint worked with palette knives into ridges and furrows that catch and reflect light differently from every angle.", "Soulages coined 'Outrenoir' (Beyond Black) for the optical phenomenon: the blacker the paint, the more light is reflected. 50 years of black paintings.", "male", "French"),
    ("Antoni Tàpies: Ochre with Four Creases", "Antoni Tàpies", 1961, "Mixed media on canvas", "Arte Informal / Spanish", ["material","textural","abstract","body","powerful"], ["ochre surface","creases","marks","material","body"], ["ochre","dark marks","cream","textured surface"], "Tàpies Foundation, Barcelona", "Thick ochre matter with four horizontal creases pressed into the surface — like the palm of a hand or scarred skin.", "Tàpies's matter paintings incorporate sand, clay, and collage into surfaces that read as skin, earth, and historical scar.", "male", "Spanish"),
    ("Jean Fautrier: Hostage Head No. 1", "Jean Fautrier", 1944, "Oil on paper on canvas", "Art Informel / French", ["informal","gestural","thick paint","abstract","wartime"], ["head","impasto","blurred face","pink mass","wartime"], ["pink","cream","grey","thick impasto"], "Centre Pompidou, Paris", "A blurred, battered head in thick pink impasto — made while listening to Nazi executions at the forest of Châtenay-Malabry.", "The Hostage series was the most powerful European response to WWII atrocity. Made under direct threat, the surfaces read as violated flesh.", "male", "French"),
    ("Yves Klein: IKB 191", "Yves Klein", 1962, "Pure pigment and synthetic resin on canvas", "Nouveau Réalisme / French", ["monochrome","blue","spiritual","transcendence","pure"], ["pure blue","monochrome","infinite blue","spiritual"], ["International Klein Blue","monochrome"], "Various collections", "A canvas entirely covered in Klein's patented International Klein Blue — a pigment of almost supernatural depth and intensity.", "Klein patented IKB as his own color. His monochrome blue canvases are spiritual fields — the immateriality of pure sensation.", "male", "French"),
    ("Niki de Saint Phalle: Black Nana", "Niki de Saint Phalle", 1968, "Painted polyester", "Neo-Dada / French", ["sculpture","bold","feminist","joyful","curvaceous"], ["nana","large female figure","colorful","joyful","powerful"], ["black","bright patterns","polka dots","curvaceous"], "Various collections", "A massive, rotund female figure in bold black with colorful pattern — exuberant, monumental, joyful female power.", "Saint Phalle's Nanas are celebrations of female physical power against the thin Western ideal. The Black Nana is her most politically charged.", "female", "French"),
    ("Jean Tinguely: Homage to New York", "Jean Tinguely", 1960, "Kinetic sculpture / performance", "Neo-Dada / Swiss", ["kinetic","self-destroying","machine","performance","neo-dada"], ["machine","gears","bicycle wheels","self-destruction","smoke"], ["grey metal","white smoke","scavenged material"], "Museum of Modern Art (garden, destroyed)", "A 23-foot self-destroying machine that played music, emitted smoke, and destroyed itself in MoMA's sculpture garden over 27 minutes.", "A critique of machine culture through the machine itself. Only the audience's photographs survive. The most spectacular Dada event of the 20th century.", "male", "Swiss"),

    # ── LAND ART & INSTALLATION ───────────────────────────────────────────
    ("Robert Smithson: Spiral Jetty", "Robert Smithson", 1970, "Basalt, earth, salt crystals, water", "Land Art / American", ["land art","spiral","lake","environmental","monumental"], ["spiral","Great Salt Lake","basalt rocks","salt","red water"], ["black basalt","red algae water","white salt"], "Great Salt Lake, Utah (permanent)", "A 1500-foot counterclockwise coil of black basalt jutting into the red brine of the Great Salt Lake.", "The most famous work of Land Art. Made from the site's own materials. Disappears and reappears as the lake level rises and falls.", "male", "American"),
    ("Walter De Maria: The Lightning Field", "Walter De Maria", 1977, "400 stainless steel poles", "Land Art / American", ["land art","grid","poles","lightning","new mexico"], ["400 steel poles","grid","desert","lightning","sublime"], ["silver steel","desert gold","blue sky"], "Quemado, New Mexico (permanent)", "400 stainless steel poles in a one-mile by one-kilometer grid across the New Mexico desert — electrifying in storms and glowing at dawn.", "Must be visited: visitors stay overnight to witness dawn and dusk light and (rarely) lightning striking the poles. The experience is the work.", "male", "American"),
    ("Christo and Jeanne-Claude: The Gates", "Christo and Jeanne-Claude", 2005, "Saffron fabric panels on steel gates", "Environmental Art", ["environmental art","temporary","public","color","park"], ["saffron curtains","Central Park","7500 gates","wind","temporary"], ["saffron orange","grey park path","winter"], "Central Park, New York (16 days only)", "7,503 saffron-colored fabric panels hung on steel gates along 23 miles of Central Park paths for 16 days in February.", "25 years in the making, it transformed Central Park into a river of saffron orange in winter grey. Attendance: 4 million in 16 days.", "male/female", "American"),
    ("James Turrell: Roden Crater", "James Turrell", 1977, "Extinct volcanic crater (ongoing work)", "Land Art / Light Art", ["light art","perceptual","cosmic","architectural","monumental"], ["crater","sky","light","naked eye astronomy","perception"], ["blue sky","earth brown","tufa"], "Painted Desert, Arizona (not yet open)", "An extinct volcano gradually transformed into a naked-eye observatory — chambers aligned to solstices, equinoxes, and planetary cycles.", "The most ambitious artwork in progress. Turrell has spent 50 years carving the crater. The light changes are perceptual, not technological.", "male", "American"),

    # ── CONTEMPORARY INTERNATIONAL ────────────────────────────────────────
    ("Gerhard Richter: Cage Paintings (series)", "Gerhard Richter", 2006, "Oil on canvas", "Painting / German", ["abstract","layered","squeegee","grey","complex"], ["layered paint","squeegee marks","grey","complex surface"], ["grey","cream","dark overpainting","squeegee textures"], "Various collections", "Multiple layers of paint pulled with a squeegee create complex, geological surfaces — named after composer John Cage.", "Richter's squeegee paintings build multiple layers of grey then expose earlier colors through dragging. The richest of his abstract works.", "male", "German"),
    ("Takashi Murakami: My Lonesome Cowboy", "Takashi Murakami", 1998, "Oil, acrylic, fiberglass, iron", "Superflat / Japanese", ["superflat","anime","sculpture","pop","japanese"], ["anime figure","lasso of fluid","sculpture","manga","pop"], ["white","vivid color","pink flesh","glossy"], "Various collections", "An anime-styled male figure creates a lasso out of a stream of bodily fluid in a glossy fiberglass sculpture.", "A signature work of Murakami's 'Superflat' theory — merging high art, anime, and consumer culture into a new Japanese visual language.", "male", "Japanese"),
    ("Olafur Eliasson: The Weather Project", "Olafur Eliasson", 2003, "Monofrequency lamps, mirrors, haze", "Installation / Danish-Icelandic", ["installation","light","immersive","artificial sun","mirror"], ["artificial sun","mirror ceiling","mist","bodies below","golden light"], ["monochrome yellow","mirror reflection","haze"], "Tate Modern Turbine Hall (temporary)", "A giant artificial sun at the end of the Turbine Hall, the ceiling mirrored so visitors lie on the floor to see themselves reflected against the 'sky'.", "2 million visitors in 6 months — the most visited Tate exhibition. Visitors became performers lying on the floor in collective solar meditation.", "male", "Danish-Icelandic"),
    ("Doris Salcedo: Shibboleth", "Doris Salcedo", 2007, "Concrete, steel (crack in floor)", "Contemporary / Colombian", ["installation","floor crack","immigration","history","conceptual"], ["crack in floor","migration","border","Turbine Hall","racism"], ["concrete grey","crack darkness"], "Tate Modern Turbine Hall (repaired, crack marks remain)", "A 548-foot crack running the length of the Tate Turbine Hall floor, deepening as it went — representing the colonial divide between Europe and the developing world.", "The crack could not be fully repaired — its traces remain. A physical embodiment of the rifts of colonialism and racism in Western culture.", "female", "Colombian"),
    ("Thomas Hirschhorn: Gramsci Monument", "Thomas Hirschhorn", 2013, "Wood, cardboard, tape, monitors", "Contemporary / Swiss", ["installation","temporary","community","political","participatory"], ["community","monument","Bronx","political theory","ephemeral"], ["plywood","cardboard","tape","temporary materials"], "Forest Houses, Bronx, New York (temporary)", "A temporary monument to Antonio Gramsci built in a Bronx housing project with community participation — a library, bar, stage, radio.", "Hirschhorn builds 'monuments' to radical thinkers in the communities they most affect. Political art as lived community space.", "male", "Swiss"),
    ("Paul Chan: Sade for Sade's Sake", "Paul Chan", 2009, "Digital video projection", "Contemporary / American", ["digital","video","projection","text","erotic"], ["text projection","shadow figures","falling bodies","erotic literature"], ["black ground","white projected text","falling silhouettes"], "Various galleries", "Scrolling text from the Marquis de Sade projected with silhouettes of falling and writhing bodies in black and white.", "Chan's projections address political violence and erotic literature as parallel exercises of power. Critical of the Iraq War context.", "male", "American"),
    ("Hito Steyerl: Liquidity Inc.", "Hito Steyerl", 2014, "HD video installation", "Video Art / German", ["video","installation","internet","liquidity","essay film"], ["water","internet","finance","MMA fighter","essay film"], ["blue screen","water imagery","screen text"], "Various galleries", "An essay film exploring water as metaphor for internet data flow, financial liquidity, and political instability.", "Steyerl's video essays are the most intellectually rigorous in contemporary art. Her analysis of image circulation in the digital age is without peer.", "female", "German"),
    ("Kara Walker: After the Deluge", "Kara Walker", 2006, "Cut-paper silhouettes", "Contemporary / American", ["silhouette","historical","race","slavery","cut paper"], ["silhouettes","flooding","Katrina","slavery","history"], ["black cut paper","white background"], "Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York", "Black cut-paper silhouettes depicting scenes from Hurricane Katrina through the lens of slavery and racial history.", "Walker's silhouettes use the 19th-century parlor art of paper cutting to expose the racial violence underneath American history.", "female", "American"),
    ("Theaster Gates: Archive", "Theaster Gates", 2014, "Salvaged materials, community", "Contemporary / American", ["social practice","salvage","community","chicago","archive"], ["fire hose","tile","library shelves","salvage","community"], ["dark wood","industrial materials","archive textures"], "Various exhibitions", "Shelves of salvaged architectural materials — tiles, fire hoses, library books — from demolished Chicago South Side buildings.", "Gates's practice transforms urban salvage into art and architecture. His Dorchester Project repurposed derelict South Side buildings as community spaces.", "male", "American"),
    ("Glenn Ligon: Untitled (I Am Not Tragically Colored)", "Glenn Ligon", 1990, "Oil stick and gesso on canvas", "Conceptual / American", ["text-based","conceptual","black identity","language","painting"], ["text","language","black","identity","gesso"], ["black text","white gesso ground","encrusted"], "Various collections", "A phrase from Zora Neale Hurston's autobiography repeated until the letters disappear into impenetrable black gesso.", "Ligon's text paintings use the progressive erasure of text as a metaphor for the invisibility of black experience in American culture.", "male", "American"),
    ("Fred Wilson: Mining the Museum", "Fred Wilson", 1992, "Found objects / institutional critique", "Conceptual / American", ["institutional critique","found objects","museum","race","juxtaposition"], ["slave shackles","Victorian silver","museum context","race","history"], ["institutional white","silver metal","historical artifacts"], "Maryland Historical Society, Baltimore", "Wilson inserted slave shackles among the Maryland Historical Society's colonial silver, making the relationship between wealth and slavery visible.", "The most important work of institutional critique in American art. Wilson 'mined' the museum's collection for what was excluded or hidden.", "male", "American"),

